Method 1: copy the value into the array and use double finger acupuncture
Ideas if you are not familiar with the linked list, the following is a summary of the list. There are two common list implementations, namely array list and linked list. If we want to store values in lists, how do we implement them? At the bottom of the array list, the array is used to store values. We can access the values at any position of the list through the index at O (1), which is based on memory addressing. The linked list stores objects called nodes. Each node stores a value and a pointer to the next node. It takes O (n) time to access the node of a specific index, because it needs to obtain the node of the next location through the pointer. It is easy to determine whether the array list is palindrome. We can use the double pointer method to compare the elements at both ends and move to the middle. One pointer moves from the starting point to the middle, and the other pointer moves from the end point to the middle. This requires o (n) time, because the time to access each element is O (1), and there are n elements to access.
However, the same method is not easy to operate on the linked list, because neither forward access nor reverse access is O (1). It is O (n) to copy the value of the linked list to the array list, so the easiest way is to copy the value of the linked list to the array list, and then use the double finger needle method to judge.
The algorithm consists of two steps:
Copy the linked list value to the array list.
Use double finger acupuncture to determine whether it is palindrome.
First, we need to traverse the linked list to copy the values into the array list. We use currentnode to point to the current node. Each iteration adds currentnode.val to the array and updates currentnode=currentnode.next. When currentnode=null, the loop stops.
The best way to perform the second step depends on the language you use. In Python, it is easy to construct a reverse copy of a list and compare two lists. In other languages, it is not so simple. Therefore, it is best to use the double pointer method to check whether it is a palindrome. We place a pointer at the beginning and a pointer at the end. Each iteration determines whether the elements pointed to by the two pointers are the same. If they are different, false is returned; If it is the same, move the two pointers inward and continue to judge until the two pointers meet. In the coding process, note that we compare the value of the node, not the node itself. The correct comparison method is: node_1.val==node_2.val, while node_1==node_2 is wrong.
class Solution {
public:
bool isPalindrome(ListNode* head) {
vector<int> vals;
while (head != nullptr) {
vals.emplace_back(head->val);
head = head->next;
}
for (int i = 0, j = (int)vals.size() - 1; i < j; ++i, --j) {
if (vals[i] != vals[j]) {
return false;
}
}
return true;
}
};
